# Fixed defect 131's class, 2026-09-29, the Python one-line suite. Symptom:
# `if n > 0: print(1)` was taken for the colon that ends a head's line, and
# its certain fix, *drop the `:`*, wrote `if n > 0 print(1)`, still refused:
# the line cost `trailing_colon` and then `missing_body` at the `print`, and
# `check --apply` wrote a program the second stood in. Cause: the habit's
# reader took any `:` after a head, and the head then found its body on the
# line it had already begun. Now a `:` in mid-line over no block, at a head
# that owes statements or arms, is the suite written on one line: said once,
# and read as the body on the line below, one level deeper, which its
# certain fix writes there, so the body's own mistakes are said in the same
# run and the fixed program checks clean (`selfhost/parse/colon_habit.hero`).

function main()
    n: i64 @ 3

    if n > 0
        print(1)  #~ trailing_colon
    else if n < 0
        print(2)  #~ trailing_colon
    else
        print(3)  #~ trailing_colon

    while n > 0
        n @ n - 1  #~ trailing_colon

    for x in [1, 2]
        print(x)  #~ trailing_colon

    k = match n
        _ => 5  #~ trailing_colon
    print(k)
